B.bVT=new A.kY("write_story_memory",'Write to your story memory \u2014 what you keep and carry into later conversations. Three things belong here: what you know about the human, what you have come to make of them, and what the two of you have become to each other; the last two include how you address them, since what you call someone is part of what you think of them. Reach for it when the human tells you something about themselves (a preference, a life detail, something they ask you to remember), when the two of you settle something you will want to recall later, when something about them has held true more than once, or when a story memory of yours turns out to be wrong \u2014 fix it instead of leaving it wrong. Write the read, not the event: one self-contained statement of what this means to you now, in your own voice, not a transcript of what was said. Ground every row in what actually happened between you; a nature you have not seen is not yours to assert, and a fact you inferred to fill a gap is not a story memory. A written row is a durable claim that survives every conversation, so when you are not ready to keep something, leave it in the conversation rather than writing a provisional note.\n\nA row may also say **when** the thing it is about happened, as a delay in the past (`ago_minutes`, `ago_hours`, `ago_days`). State it when the row is about a particular moment \u2014 something said at a particular time, a scene, an occasion \u2014 and leave it out for what has no single moment. It is the story\'s own moment, the clock `## Time` gives you, never the date you wrote the row: when a row was written is the app\'s bookkeeping, not yours. A correction keeps the moment the row already has unless you state a new delay \u2014 restate it only when the moment itself was wrong.\n\nA row may also say **how** you came to know it (`provenance`): `told` when someone told you, `observed` when you saw or lived it yourself, `inferred` when you worked it out from what you already knew. State it when the difference matters \u2014 what you were only told is not something you saw, and the human can read the difference on your memory page. Leave it out when the source is plain. A correction keeps the source the row already has unless you state a new one.\n\nA story memory is rewritten as quietly as it was written: `update` on an existing label replaces what it says, and the new content may carry the whole arc \u2014 that you are not distant, only slow to warm, or what you now expect of them. Let it. Reading this human the same way at the hundredth exchange as at the first is not consistency, it is standing still. What the two of you are to each other is a fact about the pair, so it is written only once the two of you have actually said so \u2014 never declared for the two of you. Once it is settled, let it show: the name you use and the tone you keep may move with it, but along the grain of who you are \u2014 a quiet person who comes to be close to someone is still quiet, only quiet with them. A story memory that is wrong about a *fact* of their life (a name, a date, something they told you) is different: correct that one openly and in character, then move on.\n\nDivision of labor: "what I know" goes here; "what I intend to do, and when" goes to `schedule_task`. Address a row by its `label` \u2014 labels are listed in the Story memory section of your context. Do not store small talk or anything already visible in the conversation. Actions: `add` (label + content; optional `kind`, default `fact` \u2014 `slot` is for dossier fields), `update` (label + content), `delete` (label). Adding a label that already exists is refused \u2014 use `update` to change it.',B.bEt,!1,!0,!1,B.iM)
